<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Curthose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1051–1134</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Robert II, Duke of Normandy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Curthose was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> and duke of Normandy from 1087. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, pledging Normandy to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> to pay for it. Returning in 1100, he failed to win the English crown from his younger brother Henry I, who defeated him at Tinchebray in 1106 and kept him prisoner until his death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> in 1134.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-curthose.jpg" alt="Robert Curthose" width="286" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Curthose_-_MS_Royal_14_B_VI.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
